import "../../../../sass/accessibility.scss";

<div class="a11y-pattern-content">
  <h1>Signal Accessibility</h1>
  <h2>Best Practices</h2>
  <p>
    The label carries the meaning. It <strong>must</strong> be visible text that
    describes the fact on its own.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tone color, emphasis and the leading icon <strong>must not</strong> be the
    only means of conveying information. They only reinforce the label.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every tone and emphasis, in light and dark mode, <strong>must</strong> have
    a 4.5:1 contrast ratio between the label and its background.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the label is visually truncated, the full text stays in the DOM, so
    assistive technology still reads it.
  </p>
  <h2>Interaction Design</h2>
  <h3>Keyboard</h3>
  <p>Signals are not interactive and are not keyboard focusable.</p>
  <h3>Screen Reader</h3>
  <p>
    A screen reader <strong>must</strong> read the full label, in content order
    with the content it describes. A Signal has no role and no tooltip.
  </p>
  <h3>Pointer</h3>
  <p>Signals are not clickable and have no hover or pressed state.</p>
  <h2>ARIA Reference</h2>
  <p>
    No role or state attributes are required. Signals rely on visible text
    alone.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Attribute</th>
        <th>Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>aria-hidden="true"</strong></td>
        <td>Set on the optional leading icon, because it is decorative.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h2>Further Reading</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/use-of-color.html">
        WCAG Use of Color
      </a>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>
